  <dashboards>
    <dashboard name='README'>
      <repository-location derived-from='https://tableau.deliveryhero.net/t/GlobalStandardReporting/workbooks/TableauCostSplitBasis_16686324916320/README?rev=' id='README' path='/t/GlobalStandardReporting/workbooks/TableauCostSplitBasis_16686324916320' revision='' site='GlobalStandardReporting' />
      <style>
        <style-rule element='dash-text'>
          <format attr='vertical-align' id='dash-text_1' value='top' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='800' maxwidth='1000' minheight='800' minwidth='1000' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='2' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone forceUpdate='true' h='98000' id='1' type-v2='text' w='98400' x='800' y='1000'>
            <formatted-text>
              <run bold='true' fontsize='14'>Purpose Of Report</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>This workbook is the basis for splitting Tableau Server costs across the DH entities. </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>We only consider users that have accessed Tableau (or had their account created) within the past 30 days. </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>We then assign users to entities using the below logic,</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>1) We check which countries each user has permissions to view the data of. If there is just one brand/country then we assign the user to the associated entity. For example a user with access to only Sweden data, would be assigned to Foodora Sweden.</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>2) If the user email is not empty, then we use this, otherwise we fallback to the user_name as it is mostly the employee&apos;s email address </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>3) If we can easily identify an entity from the email, e.g. foodpanda.sg = Singapore, then the employee is assigned to that entity </run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>4) If the employee has a more generic email address, e.g. talabat.com, then we assign the employee to the main cost center for the entity, e.g. Talabat Kuwait</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>5) If we have no email address data, and the employee has access to only one Site, e.g. Pedidosya, then we assign costs to the main entity for that Site</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>6) If we still cannot assign the user to an entity, we assign them to Delivery Hero SE</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
              <run fontsize='12'>Costs are split pro-rata based upon the number of users that an entity has. It is worth noting that our Tableau Server costs are somewhat static. Therefore an entity&apos;s costs can go down if their number of users goes up (if overall user numbers increase faster).</run>
              <run>Æ&#10;&#10;</run>
            </formatted-text>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <simple-id uuid='{47FEC5FC-834C-4585-BEE0-5B2CA7F3C021}' />
    </dashboard>
  </dashboards>
